{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Sqrt","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic"],"declarations":19,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.k_sq_eq_half_of_saddle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.k_sq_eq_half_of_saddle","kind":"theorem","line":434,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L434-L450","doc":"**`k = 2^{−1/2}` is forced by the saddle condition alone.**\n\n`c_σ = 2 sin θ*·|cot θ*|^{−1/2}` and `|τ| = |cot θ*|/2`, so in `k := c_σ·√|τ|`\nthe Hessian cancels *before it is evaluated*.  No `θ ∈ (π/2, π)`, no `cot θ* =\n−√3`, no trace field, no manifold — only `2 sin θ* = 1`.\n\nThis is why the `4₁` calibration was never carrying information. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.c_pow_four_eq_third","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.c_pow_four_eq_third","kind":"theorem","line":424,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L424-L432","doc":"**`c_σ = 3^{−1/4}`, with no freedom.**  Stated as `c_σ⁴ = 1/3` to stay\ninside `npow`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.c_sq_sqrt_three_of_saddle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.c_sq_sqrt_three_of_saddle","kind":"theorem","line":415,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L415-L422","doc":"`c_σ² · √3 = 1`, from the saddle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.saddle_forces_cot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.saddle_forces_cot","kind":"theorem","line":393,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L393-L413","doc":"**The saddle condition pins the cotangent.**  On `(π/2, π)`, the only\nsolution of `2 sin θ = 1` has `cot θ = −√3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_radical_is_gaussian_norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_radical_is_gaussian_norm","kind":"theorem","line":326,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L326-L331","doc":"**The radical is a Gaussian norm.**  `n² + m²` is `normSq (n − mi)`, so\n`τ²` is `16^{n−1}` times the norm of an element of `ℤ[i]` — the ring of integers\nof `ℚ(i)`, the trace field of the regular ideal octahedron. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_tau_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_tau_sq","kind":"theorem","line":313,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L313-L324","doc":"**`τ² = 16^{n−1}(n² + m²)`** — no radical survives the squaring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.cinquefoil_value_is_fib_dimension","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.cinquefoil_value_is_fib_dimension","kind":"theorem","line":281,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L281-L284","doc":"**The cinquefoil sits at the bottom of the tower.**  With `φ² = φ + 1`, the\nBring `ζ₅` carrier's Jones value `2 + φ` is `1 + φ² = D²_Fib`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.keystone_core_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.keystone_core_identity","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L275-L279","doc":"The keystone's core `ℚ(√5)` identity, `(10 − 2√5)(10 + 2√5) = 80`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.marks_sq_sum_eq_120","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.marks_sq_sum_eq_120","kind":"theorem","line":269,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L269-L273","doc":"**`Σ marks² = 120 = |2I|`** — the binary icosahedral group's order, and\n`|S₅|`, and `|Aut(Bring)|`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.keystone_dimension_tower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.keystone_dimension_tower","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L260-L267","doc":"**The keystone tower.**  `D²_{SU(2)₃} = 2 D²_Fib` and `D²_{Ê₈} = 4 D²_Fib`,\nfor every `φ` — the ratios come from the mark multiset, not from `√5`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.golden_quantum_dimension","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.golden_quantum_dimension","kind":"theorem","line":255,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L255-L258","doc":"**`[2] = 2cos(π/5) = φ`.**  The tower's input is a theorem, not a table. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.qdim5","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.qdim5","kind":"def","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L245-L253","doc":"Quantum dimensions at `r = 5`, on the affine `Ê₈` McKay marks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_c_sigma_iff_tau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdv_c_sigma_iff_tau","kind":"theorem","line":185,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L185-L212","doc":"**The derived constant forces the torsion.**\n\n`c_σ = 2^{1/2−n}(n²+m²)^{−1/4}` together with the law `c_σ = √(1/(2τ))` holds\nexactly when `τ = 2^{2n−2}√(n²+m²)`, written here as `4ⁿ√(n²+m²)/4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.law_inverts","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.law_inverts","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L167-L183","doc":"**The law inverts.**  For positive `τ` and `c`, `c = √(1/(2τ))` and\n`τ = 1/(2c²)` say the same thing.  Stated separately because it is the only\nstep in `vdv_c_sigma_iff_tau` that is about the law rather than the constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdvCSigma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.vdvCSigma","kind":"def","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L163-L165","doc":"The corpus's 1-loop law in the form it is actually used: `c = √(1/(2τ))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.normalisation_step_trivial_of_k_agree","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.normalisation_step_trivial_of_k_agree","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L121-L138","doc":"**What a `k`-agreement bounds.**\n\nIf the measured constant differs from the true one by an unknown `c`-dependent\ndictionary step, `k_meas c = k_true * g c` with `k_true ≠ 0`, then agreement of\nthe MEASURED constants at two cusp numbers forces the step to agree there.\n\nThis is what licences reading the 17-digit `k₁ = k₂` agreement as a constraint\non the missing normalisation — it excludes every candidate `g` with\n`g 1 ≠ g 2`, which is how `2^{(c-1)/2}`, `2^{c-1}`, `c`, `√c` and `c^{1/4}` were\nruled out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.k_const_iff_exponent_neg_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.k_const_iff_exponent_neg_half","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L81-L119","doc":"**`k`-constancy and `p = −1/2` are the same statement.**\n\nThe corpus checked that `k_c := c_σ·|τ|^{1/2}` agrees across manifolds and\nlisted the exponent as assumed.  Given two distinct positive `τ` values, the\nagreement holds exactly when the two-point exponent is `−1/2`; the check was an\nexponent measurement all along. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:one-loop-prefactor-provenance","label":"prop:one-loop-prefactor-provenance","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/one-loop-prefactor-provenance.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.exponent_normalisation_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.exponent_normalisation_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L67-L79","doc":"**The exponent is invariant under an overall rescaling of `c`.**\n\nRescaling `c ↦ A·c` is exactly what a change of normalisation convention does\nto the observable, and it cancels in the numerator: `log (A·c₁) − log (A·c₂) =\nlog c₁ − log c₂`.  So a convention offset can move the prefactor `k` freely and\ncan never move `p`.  No positivity is needed on `t₁, t₂` — the denominator is\nuntouched. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.twoPointExponent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.twoPointExponent","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/OneLoopPrefactorInvariance.lean#L62-L65","doc":"The two-point exponent of a power law through `(t₁, c₁)` and `(t₂, c₂)`:\nthe unique `p` with `cᵢ = k·tᵢ^p` for some `k`, read off in logarithms. ","signatureChunk":0}]}